1
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:32,960
With statements like "what doesn't kill me makes me stronger", "God is dead!
And we have killed him! "

2
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:38,680
And "the world is beautiful but man is a disease" is his philosophy both tickling and provocative.

3
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:44,540
That is probably why more than 20,000 different books have been published about him and his writings.

4
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:50,840
He was born in 1844 in a small village in the eastern part of Germany where his father was a priest.

5
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:57,000
He was considered a wonderful child and became a professor at the University of Basel at the age of 24.

6
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:59,340
However, his career was not successful.

7
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:04,360
He got tired of his colleagues, gave up his job and moved to the Swiss Alps

8
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:06,980
Where he lived alone and wrote on his books.

9
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:11,120
He was sickly, opium dependent and disliked his family:

10
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:15,800
"I don't like my mother and it's painful to hear as much as hearing my sister's voice".

11
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:18,980
Women rejected him and his books did not sell.

12
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:24,340
Only 44 years old, he had a mental breakdown when he saw a horse being whipped by the cousin.

13
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:28,740
He ran to embrace the horse screaming "I understand you!".

14
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:36,180
He never recovered, sank deep into madness and spent the remainder of his life at a mental hospital.

15
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:43,380
Furthermore, he was vegetarian, abstinent in terms of coffee and alcohol, and had a crazy big mustache.

16
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:49,220
He was strongly influenced by Schopenhauer's thoughts that the world we perceive is our own performance,

17
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:51,760
Thus, a purely subjective experience.

18
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:57,080
Although he came to deviations from his philosophy, his perception of his life remained

19
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,680
Related to Schopenhauer's pessimism.

20
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:06,260
For Nietzsche, philosophy was not only abstract theories, but had a practical purpose.

21
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:14,380
The philosophy was intended to facilitate the emergence of a new and better type of man who devoted his life to development,

22
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:20,620
self -realization and life -affirming - a man who was not held back by moral views

23
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:25,240
Like right and wrong, a man who affirmed the animal in itself.

24
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:29,540
Nietzsche saw himself as a guide for such a person.

25
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:37,160
“Only these are my readers, my rightful readers, my predetermined readers.
What does the rest matter?

26
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,040
The rest is just people. "

27
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:43,860
Central to Nietzsche's philosophy is the concept of "the will to power"

28
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:47,980
which should be seen as a response to Schopenhauer's "the will to live".

29
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:53,200
Where Schopenhauer believes that everything in the universe is an expression of the will to live, Nietzsche said that

30
00:02:53,200 --> 00:03:00,600
The main driving force is not life but power - "an inexhaustible, generating vitality".

31
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:07,100
According to him, the operation is to expand his power stronger than the operation to adapt and survive.

32
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:12,000
This will to power is wherever there is life, and even the strongest of creatures

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,680
will risk their lives in their quest for even more power.

34
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:20,540
Unlike Schopenhauer, however, Nietzsche does not see the will as a negative force

35
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:24,920
Without something to affirm even if it can sometimes be destructive.

36
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:29,400
While Schopenhauer sees the pity as a positive resort,

37
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:01,420
Nietzsche had extreme things to say about Christianity.

38
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:08,640
"Throughout the New Testament, there is only one person worth respecting: Pilate, the Roman governor".

39
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:13,780
According to the Bible, Pontius Pilate was the person who decided to crucify Jesus.

40
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:19,760
Christianity, in Nietzsche's interpretation, had arisen from Roman slaves who had lacked the power to achieve

41
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:24,760
What they really wanted and instead created a philosophy that made virtue of their cowardice.

42
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:31,600
Christians, whom he called "Die Herde" - the flock, also wanted to enjoy life (intellectual challenges,

43
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:39,160
Creativity, six mm) but were too incapable to achieve a high level of pleasure and satisfaction.

44
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:44,540
Therefore, they joined a hypocritical faith where they condemned what they wanted but were too weak

45
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:49,140
To achieve - and paid tribute to what they happened to have, but did not demand.

46
00:05:49,620 --> 00:05:56,980
In the Christian value system, sexual inability became purity.
Weakness became goodness.
Apathy became patience,

47
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:00,180
And inability to revenge was forgiveness.

48
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:05,780
According to Nietzsche, Christianity was a gigantic machine of bitter denial.

49
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:11,700
"After coming into contact with a religious man, I always feel that I have to wash my hands."

50
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:45,300
The moralists, with Socrates and Jesus at the forefront, then came and said that this development process was wrong.

51
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:50,140
They claimed that there should be laws that protect the weak against the strong ones.

52
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:57,580
They meant that justice would go before strength, that the earth would belong to the lazy, not the entrepreneurs.

53
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:02,340
“Independent thinking and powerful intellect is considered dangerous;

54
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:07,880
Everything that lifts an individual over the mass scares, and is henceforth referred to as evil. "

55
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:13,060
In every human being there is an instinct Nietzsche calls flock behavior,

56
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:15,340
A need to adapt to the masses.

57
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:22,020
Often this comes as an expression in adapting your morals, ie what you think is right and wrong in society.

58
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:26,560
"Morality is the best of all devices for leading humanity in ruin"

59
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:29,120
Nietzsche proclaimed in the book "Antichrist".

60
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:34,640
Such a morality, which was accepted by the masses, called Nietzsche for slave morality.

61
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:37,840
He meant that this morality served a clear purpose

62
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:44,900
- Giving mediocre individuals the conviction that their weakness is not an error, but a strength.

63
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:50,660
"I've often laughed at these wavers who thought they were good just because they were missing chlorine,"

64
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:53,560
Nietzsche wrote in "So Zarathustra".

65
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:58,960
In this way, slave morality claims that properties it itself lack are evil.

66
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:02,360
Through the slave morality, they gain weak respect.

67
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:08,340
Sheep-like qualities are hailed as good, and individuals are thus pressed to become weak and obedient.

68
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:14,360
Therefore, we must, Nietzsche, flee the shackles of the slave morality and in its place create a new one,

69
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:18,620
Independent and life -affirming morals - the male.

70
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:24,360
We have to fight the slave moral as the disease it is, but in order to succeed in this one must first

71
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:28,980
Physically separated from the shock and live a life in loneliness.

72
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:34,660
Nietzsche meant that weak people structure their lives so they never have to reflect

73
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:37,260
around the difficult questions of existence.
